From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

(I will try to make this my _last_ post this go round on the
information-theoretic approach to gambling and investments!)

Richard Kennaway asks an important question that tends to get buried in the
details:

>How is the analysis of these and other stock-market games affected if you
>don't maximise asymptotic growth, but instead maximise a constant income
>which you take out of the game on each turn?  Or an exponentially growing
>income, to finance an ever more expansive and expensive lifestyle?

I won't get into exponentially growing income (great if you can get it!)
vs. constant income, but I want to look at 3 situations for any investment
and what ought to be done:

* For "superfair" odds, where the odds of winning are better than fair, the
optimum strategy is always to have _all_ of one's money invested. How much
you take out depends on how much gain you want to give up for that income.
Generally, keep as much invested or in the game as you can.

* For "fair" odds, all kinds of strategies exist. Taking cash out really
doesn't matter in the long run (as you're not making money anyway, on
average).

* For "subfair" odds, where the odds are less than fair, the best strategy
is to get out, to not play! However, if you must play, then "It is usually
desirable to bet only some of the money and leave the rest aside as cash.
Proportional gambling is no longer log-optimal." (Cover and Thomas, p. 130)

Remember the First and Second Laws of Thermodynamics, Gambling Style:

1. You can't win.

2. You can't even break even.

The connections between entropy, order, predictability, gambling,
complexity, and the laws of thermodynamics are really quite profound.

-Tim May

