>that being an open anarchist is a risk, albeit a small one, to my life.
>I made a somewhat smaller similar decision previous to that on joining
>the Libertarian Party -- I realized that I was doing something that might
>later get me hauled in front of a congressional committee and decided
>that I was willing to risk it.
...more commments elided....

Good comments. I suppose I figure that if I couch some of my comments in
humorous terms, as I sometimes do, that even the fools in BATF will know
I'm not actually a truly dangerous gun nut (not that having guns makes one
a danger to anyone). But perhaps not.

One reason I like to post to this list, and to the Cypherpunks list, is
that I don't fear the "permanent records syndrome" as much. Let me explain.

All postings to Usenet are essentially archived in many sites. Usenet
traffic is available on CD-ROM and, someone just told me, by anonymous ftp
from some sites that maintain huge archives. (This came up in a posting I
made in news.admin.policy, which I'll include at the end of these
comments.)

Thus, nearly anyone can retrieve _all_ of your postings, no matter how
ill-conceived, or angry, or bizarre, to any of the several thousand Usenet
groups. Perhaps you posted to rec.nude, to alt.sex.furry.animals, to
soc.anarchy, to alt.drugs, or to rec.guns. You get the picture. 

Future employers may routinely call up "credit checks" by retrieving sorted
lists of postings--I suspect government agencies already do it. In a few
years, with Gopher- and WAIS-type services, and with storage costs so
trivial, these postings will be a valuable source of info for all kinds of
purposes.

(In case anyone is wondering, I'm not suggesting any limits on this use.
For example, I keep lots of postings from various people, so any law that
limited my use of this information would limit my own use of my own files.
But I won't get into this here.)

For now at least, private mailing lists are a bit more circumspect. Someday
these postings will somehow make it into the same archival sites, though
there may be enough legal obstacles to slow this down somewhat. (Speaking
more fancifully, if either the Extropians or Cypherpunks turns out to have
an important effect, then future scholars will of course pore over these
postings, obtained somehow. <Hello future scholar! Tell me, has the
Singularity occurred? Did Hillary Rodham ever become Presidentess?
Inquiring past minds want to know!>)

As I've said before, I feel more linkage, more connection, to my friends on
these lists than to the tens of thousands of strangers reading NetNews.
Frankly, I'm more willing to spend 30 minutes writing an essay that will be
seen by 200 Extropians--and perhaps read closely by 10 of them--than I am
to write a post that will go out to some Usenet group and perhaps be seen,
albeit briefly, by thousands of strangers and barely read at all by _any_
of them.

-Tim May

