>Is keeping the body in a constant state of infection a desirable technique
>for warding off other diseases? Is it any more desireable when we talk about
>the psyche instead of the body?

In an ideal world (or even a slight improvement on the present one!) 
no.  But in the real world, there are *many* examples where having one 
sort of parasite protects you from something worse.  The only 
biological one I can think of at the moment is that being infected 
with herpes simples (cold sores) results in a certain amount of 
resistance to the (much worse) genital variety. 

For reasons I have speculated on before (ask, and I can either send a 
copy or repost a 20k article), people seem to have a "religious meme 
receptor site."  Various memes contend for the site, much the way 
various chemical signals contend for sites on a cell surface.  If your 
"religious meme receptor site" is filled with some meme in this class, 
you will be more resistant to another one attaching.  I have come to 
appreciate the common religions in this light.  Regardless of how 
harmful they may be when they start, (as cults) the ones that survive 
do not cause too much damage to their hosts. 

Or, as I put it in another article, it is safer to be a Methodist than 
to be vulnerable to a potentially fatal cult.

