Date: Wed, 3 Mar 93 09:26:13 -0800
From: cappello%cs@hub.ucsb.edu (Peter Cappello)
Subject: POLI: Free speech?

>Why should territorial property rights carry the right to stipulate  
>what actions may be performed there by other people, even when such  
>actions do not harm anyone?

Harm is a function of personal values.  Who am I to say that the  
property owner is [not] harmed (directly or otherwise) when someone  
performs some act.

The owner consents to permit the person on the premises.   The owner  
can withdraw such consent for reasons that you and I might think  
arbitrary.  To deny this, is to deny the notion of trespass.  


>It's entirely reasonable for the owner of such premises to demand a
>reasonable standard of decorum there, in the interest of protecting  
>his business or personal reputation.  However, I am concerned at the  
>use of what seems to me an excessively general principle to justify  
>this.

I share your concern.  Please introduce a less general principle  
through which we can obtain the same result.  


It may be of some comfort that, for business reasons, property owners  
are likely to be more sensitive to reasonableness arguments than  
statist institutions.  That is, in spite of the seemingly excessive  
generality of property rights, the market imposes a discipline on  
owners' use of them.  The feedback loop of the market is tighter than  
that of coercive institutions.

